{
  "intro": "Use this short script to open the debrief and keep it focused and supportive.",
  "sections": {
    "strengths": {
      "title": "Concrete analogy and clear terms",
      "body": "Point out the moment the teacher made the idea tangible and used accurate vocabulary.",
      "say_this": "I liked how you used the cake example — \"For example, think this is a cake... when your mother brings a cake, what do you do?\" — that made partition and equal shares concrete for the class."
    },
    "growth": {
      "title": "Differentiate the independent task",
      "body": "Name the single teaching move: give students different practice pathways during the independent writing/test phase so the task matches varied readiness.",
      "say_this": "When you assigned, \"You have to write proper fractions,\" consider offering a scaffolded task and an extension so students who need support practice with guided examples while others apply the idea more deeply."
    },
    "action": {
      "title": "Try labeled task lanes in the independent phase",
      "body": "Agree a concrete classroom change: teacher prepares a scaffolded practice and an extension, assigns students deliberately to each lane, and officer will observe that arrangement in the next visit.",
      "say_this": "Let's try this in the independent work: give a scaffolded practice task and a separate extension task, tell specific students which to do, and I will observe that arrangement on my next visit."
    }
  },
  "reflection_question": "How will you decide in class which students do the scaffolded task and which do the extension so everyone can make progress?",
  "outro": "Close by naming the clear strength you observed (accurate explanations and the cake analogy) and the simple instructional move to try (differentiated task lanes for independent work)."
}
